        Case(
            "longest talk kubecon-cloudnativecon-north-america-2026:1362584",
            "Demo Theater: From Wide Open to Locked Down: Running AI Agents on Kubernetes\u000AMore teams are running AI agents on Kubernetes, but many first deployments give agents far more than they need: API keys in environment variables, a mounted ServiceAccount token, open internet access, a shared node kernel, and no resource limits. This demo starts inside that kind of pod, shows what the agent can read and reach, then locks it down with five controls: gVisor isolation through agent-sandbox, a dedicated ServiceAccount, a credential broker, egress NetworkPolicy enforced by Cilium, and per-session limits and revocation. Most of the time goes to credentials: how the pod proves its identity with a ServiceAccount token, how the broker adds API keys to outbound requests so they never enter the agent's environment, and how access ends when the pod is deleted. It closes with common pitfalls and ways to extend the setup. Projects covered: Kubernetes, agent-sandbox (Kubernetes SIG Apps), Cilium, and gVisor, with Infisical Agent Vault as the credential broker.\u000A\u000A_In order to facilitate networking and business relationships at the event, you may choose to visit a third party\u2019s booth or access sponsored content. You are never required to visit third party booths or to access sponsored content. When visiting a booth or participating in sponsored activities, the third party will receive some of your registration data. This data includes your first name, last name, title, company, address, email, standard demographics questions (i.e. job function, industry), and details about the sponsored content or resources you interacted with. If you choose to interact with a booth or access sponsored content, you are explicitly consenting to receipt and use of such data by the third-party recipients, which will be subject to their own privacy policies._",
