        Case(
            "longest talk kubecon-cloudnativecon-north-america-2026:1362628",
            "Demo Theater: KubeHound: Identifying Attack Paths in Kubernetes Clusters at Scale\u000AKubeHound is an open source tool developed by Datadog that discovers attack paths in Kubernetes clusters. Security scanners give you lists: 14 container escapes, 32 privilege escalations, 72 lateral movements. But a list cannot answer the question that matters most: which of those findings can be used to access critical assets in your cluster?  \u000A  \u000AKubeHound takes a different approach. Instead of treating each finding as an isolated problem, KubeHound builds a graph showing how misconfigurations\u2014like an over-privileged service account or a hostPath mount\u2014chain together into exploitable attack paths.  \u000A  \u000AThis is how an attacker sees your cluster: not as a list of problems, but as a map of opportunities.  \u000A  \u000AThe speaker will demo how KubeHound works, connecting common misconfigurations to industry-standard MITRE ATT&CK techniques, and chaining those attacks together. Attendees will leave ready to run KubeHound on their own clusters\u2014the same tool used by security teams across Datadog.\u000A\u000A_In order to facilitate networking and business relationships at the event, you may choose to visit a third party\u2019s booth or access sponsored content. You are never required to visit third party booths or to access sponsored content. When visiting a booth or participating in sponsored activities, the third party will receive some of your registration data. This data includes your first name, last name, title, company, address, email, standard demographics questions (i.e. job function, industry), and details about the sponsored content or resources you interacted with. If you choose to interact with a booth or access sponsored content, you are explicitly consenting to receipt and use of such data by the third-party recipients, which will be subject to their own privacy policies._",
