        Case(
            "three longest talks joined",
            "Demo Theater: YugabyteDB AMP: Agentic Multitenant PostgreSQL for the AI Era\u000AAI agents are bursty, mostly idle, and unpredictable at scale. Traditional databases charge you for idle capacity and buckle when one runaway agent hogs shared resources. Yet AI agents still need high availability, resilience, and constant tuning, and the ones that reach production often outgrow a single node.\u000AYugabyteDB AMP (Agentic Multitenant Postgres) is built for that journey. It manages large database fleets, each growing from a fraction of a core to a distributed, multi-continent deployment, with no migration or re-architecture along the way.\u000AJoin the YugabyteDB team for a first look at AMP. You\u2019ll learn:\u000AHow to spin up PostgreSQL clusters with an agent, MCP, the API, or the UI, and branch onto independent compute with zero-copy thin cloning\u000AHow AMP\u2019s idle databases scale to zero, so small and early-stage workloads don't pay for idle capacity \u000AHow four agents (Architect, Voyager, Perf Advisor, and Nexus) automate setup, migration, integrations, and performance tuning\u000A\u000A_In order to facilitate networking and business relationships at the event, you may choose to visit a third party\u2019s booth or access sponsored content. You are never required to visit third party booths or to access sponsored content. When visiting a booth or participating in sponsored activities, the third party will receive some of your registration data. This data includes your first name, last name, title, company, address, email, standard demographics questions (i.e. job function, industry), and details about the sponsored content or resources you interacted with. If you choose to interact with a booth or access sponsored content, you are explicitly consenting to receipt and use of such data by the third-party recipients, which will be subject to their own privacy policies._\u000A\u000ADemo Theater: From Wide Open to Locked Down: Running AI Agents on Kubernetes\u000AMore teams are running AI agents on Kubernetes, but many first deployments give agents far more than they need: API keys in environment variables, a mounted ServiceAccount token, open internet access, a shared node kernel, and no resource limits. This demo starts inside that kind of pod, shows what the agent can read and reach, then locks it down with five controls: gVisor isolation through agent-sandbox, a dedicated ServiceAccount, a credential broker, egress NetworkPolicy enforced by Cilium, and per-session limits and revocation. Most of the time goes to credentials: how the pod proves its identity with a ServiceAccount token, how the broker adds API keys to outbound requests so they never enter the agent's environment, and how access ends when the pod is deleted. It closes with common pitfalls and ways to extend the setup. Projects covered: Kubernetes, agent-sandbox (Kubernetes SIG Apps), Cilium, and gVisor, with Infisical Agent Vault as the credential broker.\u000A\u000A_In order to facilitate networking and business relationships at the event, you may choose to visit a third party\u2019s booth or access sponsored content. You are never required to visit third party booths or to access sponsored content. When visiting a booth or participating in sponsored activities, the third party will receive some of your registration data. This data includes your first name, last name, title, company, address, email, standard demographics questions (i.e. job function, industry), and details about the sponsored content or resources you interacted with. If you choose to interact with a booth or access sponsored content, you are explicitly consenting to receipt and use of such data by the third-party recipients, which will be subject to their own privacy policies._\u000A\u000ADemo Theater: Why Your Kubernetes Autoscalers Keep Fighting (and How to End the Thrash)\u000AAsk your cluster a simple question: what is the right size for checkout-api, and how many replicas should it run right now? HPA says 11. KEDA says 24. VPA says the pods are over-provisioned. Karpenter is adding nodes sized to requests nobody uses. And Argo CD wants it all reset to whatever is in Git.\u000AEach controller is doing its job. Together, they thrash. In this demo, we'll talk live about the four collisions: the HPA/VPA denominator collapse, the HPA/KEDA multi-writer race, GitOps drift ping-pong, and the node-layer multiplier behind fleets that average roughly 8% CPU utilization.\u000AThen we show a different approach: an autonomous decision layer that reads every signal at once, computes replicas and requests together, pre-warms ahead of predictable peaks, and writes its changes back to Git as an explained pull request. Your autoscalers keep doing what they do well. Something finally decides what they should do.\u000A\u000A\u000A_In order to facilitate networking and business relationships at the event, you may choose to visit a third party\u2019s booth or access sponsored content. You are never required to visit third party booths or to access sponsored content. When visiting a booth or participating in sponsored activities, the third party will receive some of your registration data. This data includes your first name, last name, title, company, address, email, standard demographics questions (i.e. job function, industry), and details about the sponsored content or resources you interacted with. If you choose to interact with a booth or access sponsored content, you are explicitly consenting to receipt and use of such data by the third-party recipients, which will be subject to their own privacy policies._",
